# DTF tier manifest: bounded-download-dispatch
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. No SSO/S3 needed -- the oracle drives the
# real hosted-download hot path (maven GET) over HTTP, injects a slow
# event-store sink via a pg_sleep trigger, and reads ground truth from
# pg_stat_activity + download_statistics via docker exec psql.
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS (#2522, bounded half): the first #2522 slice moved the two
# download side-effect writes (download_statistics INSERT, ARTIFACT_DOWNLOADED
# audit INSERT) off the byte plane with a per-request `tokio::spawn`. That left
# them UNBOUNDED: every download spawned a detached task holding a catalog-pool
# connection, with no cap and no backpressure. Under a download flood with a
# slow/failing event store, tasks + pool connections grow without bound
# (pool max_connections is 50 by default -- a flood pins ~all of them on
# telemetry INSERTs), and the exhausted pool couples back into serving. The fix
# replaces the spawns with ONE bounded mpsc queue + a FIXED pool of flush
# workers (default 2) that batch-INSERT, shedding (drop + metric) on overflow.
#
# OBSERVABLE CONTRACT (discriminating oracle):
#   With the event store made slow (BEFORE INSERT pg_sleep trigger on
#   download_statistics), a flood of F concurrent GETs must
#     (1) all return 200 with the exact bytes, promptly (byte plane decoupled),
#     (2) never hold more than WORKER_BOUND concurrent backends in an active
#         `INSERT INTO download_statistics` (measured via pg_stat_activity
#         sampling during the flood) -- the bounded property itself,
#     (3) still land every row eventually once the sink recovers (bounded-retry
#         poll; batch flush timing), and a HEAD must land zero rows.
#
#   * GREEN on the fixed image: max concurrent stats-INSERT backends stays at
#     or below the flush-worker bound (2 workers; asserted <= 4 for margin).
#   * RED on the unbounded (main-reference) image: each GET spawns its own
#     INSERT which sleeps in the trigger, so the sampled concurrency climbs
#     toward min(F, pool_max) >> 4 -> FAIL. This was validated red->green
#     against a main-@a791799 image before the fix PR.
#
# LOSS-AMPLIFICATION LEGS (red-team follow-up on the fix itself): batching
# must not turn one bad row into batch-wide loss. (a) A 601-char User-Agent
# (vs VARCHAR(512)) is clamped at capture/insert-build, so a crafted request
# can no longer 22001-fail the batched INSERT and drop co-batched rows — all
# rows land, the long UA lands truncated to 512. (b) A row-level poison the
# clamp cannot prevent (injected RAISE trigger) loses ONLY itself: the
# split-batch fallback persists every innocent neighbor and the loss is
# counted in ak_download_events_dropped_total{reason="flush_failed"}.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
